//! read from the vector graphics of the datasheet's PDF.
//!
//! Generated by `tools/film-curves/extract_curves.py` from `fujifilm_af3_0261e_fujifilm_200.json`; do
//! not edit by hand. Source PDF:
//! <https://www.ishootfujifilm.com/uploads/FUJIFILM%20200%20Data%20Sheet.pdf>
//! (SHA-256 `afd2ef317d21e119545b4401f10b6438312f61dcab82e28ee609724a8b0dcc02`).
//!
//! Characteristic curves: daylight, 1/125 s, Process CN-16 (Fujifilm's C-41), Status M
//! densitometry. Fujifilm draws each as a few smooth Bézier segments. The sheet gives no
//! Log H ref: -1.14 is assumed, where Kodak's ISO 200 sheets put a grey card (log10(0.8 /
//! 200) + 1.26).
//!
//! Spectral sensitivity: CN-16, Status M, density 1.0 above D-min. Fujifilm plots it
//! relative: the log axis has a 1.0 scale bar but no zero, so its gridlines are numbered 2
//! and 3 here, arbitrarily. Only the curves' shapes and their offsets from each other are
//! used (each layer is normalised to daylight on white). The curves stop where Fujifilm
//! stops drawing them.
//!
//! Spectral dye density: typical densities of a mid-scale neutral subject and D-min. The
//! 700 nm gridline is drawn 1.6 pt off the others' spacing; the axis is calibrated from 400
//! and 600 nm.
//!
//! Page coordinates: the plots' axes are calibrated from their gridlines.

